+## Root cause
+
+A completion-toggle state change re-rendered the list with `todoList.innerHTML = ...`, detaching
+and recreating the `<li>` nodes. A browser (Cypress) that holds a reference before the click sees
+the old node detach, so a chained `cy.find()` fails. Fix: mutate the existing node in place (toggle
+its `completed` class and checkbox) when the item stays visible under the current filter
+(`completeTodo` / `toggleAllTodos` under the "all" route).
+
+## Durable artifacts this leg
+
+- `features/todo-dom-stability.feature` already pins the behaviour (Rule: "The app mutates an
+  existing list element in place when an item's completion changes, instead of rebuilding the
+  list"). The first two scenarios map 1:1 to the two acceptance failures:
+  - `Completing a todo keeps the same element in the DOM`  -> failure 1
+  - `Uncompleting a todo keeps the same element in the DOM` -> failure 2
+  The third scenario, `Editing a todo hides its label`, is intentionally LEFT OFF this leg's
+  watchbill (not in the acceptance output).
+- `watchbill.json` is re-established with `watch1` referencing exactly those two scenarios. (It was
+  struck as spent after the prior leg implemented the in-place fix; this leg re-pins the behaviour
+  as QM's regression target.)
+
+## Falsifiability (verified, not assumed)
+
+Captain verified with a happy-dom micro-test that node identity is NOT preserved across an
+`innerHTML` rebuild (`held !== re-queried` after rebuild) but IS preserved by in-place mutation
+(`held === re-queried`). Therefore the in-harness scenario is genuinely falsifiable against a
+defective `innerHTML`-rebuild baseline and needs NO source-level conformance check. The step
+definitions `I hold a reference to the todo {string} element`,
+`the todo {string} element is the same element as the held reference`, and `I complete the todo
+{string}` already exist in `features/support/steps.js`, and `findTodoLiByIdentity` re-queries the
+DOM so a rebuilt list yields a different node than the held reference.
 
